  "result": "I implemented `slugify` in `src/strings.js` and added tests to `test/strings.test.js`. `npm test` passes all 15 tests: the 2 that were already there and 13 new ones.\n\n**How it works:**\n1. It lowercases the text and splits accented letters into a base letter plus accent marks, then removes the marks. That turns \"ế\" into \"e\", \"ñ\" into \"n\", and so on.\n2. Some letters don't split that way, so a small lookup table handles them. It covers đ/Đ → `d`, as you asked. I also added a few other Latin letters you didn't list: ø → `o`, ł → `l`, ß → `ss`, æ → `ae`, œ → `oe`, þ → `th`, ð → `d`, and dotless ı → `i`. Without these, those letters would turn into hyphens.\n3. Every run of characters that aren't a-z or 0-9 becomes one hyphen, and leading and trailing hyphens are removed.\n4. The slug is cut to 50 characters, and any hyphen left at the end of the cut is removed.\n\n**The new tests cover:** Vietnamese text, đ/Đ, French and Spanish accents, accented input whose marks are already stored separately, collapsing runs of symbols, digits, leading and trailing junk, empty and symbols-only input, the 50-character limit, a cut that lands on a hyphen, a slug of exactly 50 characters, and a long Vietnamese sentence.",
